@page
@model CSharpMasteryHub.Pages.AspNetCoreRestApis.JsonAndSerializationModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "JSON & Serialization";
}

<div class="container">
    <div class="row">
        <div class="col-lg-9">
            <div class="lesson-content">
                <h1 class="display-4 mb-3">JSON & Serialization</h1>
                <div class="d-flex gap-3 mb-4">
                    <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">Intermediate</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">15 min read</span>
                    <span class="badge bg-primary">Lesson 5 of 10</span>
                </div>

                <div class="alert alert-warning">
                    <i class="fas fa-language me-2"></i> <strong>The Translator Story:</strong> Imagine a French chef (C# Server) trying to talk to a Spanish customer (JavaScript Browser). They don't understand each other! They need a translator. <strong>JSON</strong> is that translator. It is a simple text format that both languages understand perfectly.
                </div>

                <h2>Connecting to C#</h2>
                <p>In C#, you work with Objects and Classes. The browser doesn't know what a C# Class is. So, we must convert our C# objects into a string format called <strong>JSON</strong>. This process is called <strong>Serialization</strong>.</p>
                <p>Converting JSON back into a C# object is called <strong>Deserialization</strong>.</p>

                <h2>What does JSON look like?</h2>
                <p>It looks exactly like a C# Dictionary or an anonymous object!</p>
<pre><code>{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "Ali",
  "isDeveloper": true,
  "skills": ["C#", "SQL", "ASP.NET"]
}</code></pre>

                <h2>Example 1: Serializing C# to JSON</h2>
<pre><code>using System.Text.Json;

public class User 
{
    public int Id { get; set; }
    public string Name { get; set; }
    public bool IsDeveloper { get; set; }
}

// Inside your code:
var myUser = new User { Id = 1, Name = "Ali", IsDeveloper = true };

// Convert C# Object to JSON string
string jsonString = JsonSerializer.Serialize(myUser);
Console.WriteLine(jsonString); 
// Output: {"Id":1,"Name":"Ali","IsDeveloper":true}</code></pre>

                <h2>Example 2: Deserializing JSON to C#</h2>
<pre><code>string incomingJson = "{\"Id\":2,\"Name\":\"Sarah\",\"IsDeveloper\":false}";

// Convert JSON string back to a C# Object
User newUser = JsonSerializer.Deserialize&lt;User&gt;(incomingJson);

Console.WriteLine(newUser.Name); // Output: Sarah</code></pre>

                <p>In ASP.NET Core APIs, you rarely have to do this manually! When you return an object like `return Ok(myUser)`, ASP.NET Core automatically serializes it to JSON for you.</p>

